==English==
{{was wotd|2008|September|9}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|à propos|àpropos}}

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|fr|à propos||on that subject}}.

Similar in meaning and form, and to some extent etymology, to {{m|en|appropriate}}, but not a doublet of it.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˌæp.ɹəˈpəʊ/|a=UK}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˌæp.ɹəˈpoʊ/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-apropos.ogg|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-apropos.ogg|a=AU}}
* {{rhymes|en|əʊ|s=3}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Of an [[appropriate]] or [[pertinent]] nature.
#: {{syn|en|apposite#Adjective|apt#Adjective|to the point|Thesaurus:pertinent}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1877|author=w:Jules Verne|translator=Frederick Amadeus Malleson|title=w:Journey into the Interior of the Earth|section=[[s:Journey_into_the_Interior_of_the_Earth/Chapter_VI|Chapter VI]]
|passage=Nothing easier. I received not long ago a map from my friend, Augustus Petermann, at Leipzig. Nothing could be more '''apropos'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Anne Valdespino|title=Mr. Stox|journal={{w|Orange Coast (magazine)|Orange Coast}}|year=2008|month=December|volume=34|issue=12|issn=0279-0483|page={{gbooks|BPwDAAAAMBAJ|139|apropos}}|passage=Served outside the shell and sliced in bite-sized pieces, it's as '''apropos''' for a first date as a business dinner.}}
# [[by the way|By the way]], [[incidental]].
#* {{RQ:Doyle Study in Scarlet|page=14|passage={{w|Sherlock Holmes}} rose and lit his pipe. "No doubt you think that you are complimenting me in comparing me to [[w:C. Auguste Dupin|{{quote-gloss|C. Auguste}} Dupin]]," he observed. "Now, in my opinion, Dupin was a very inferior fellow. That trick of his of breaking in on his friends' thoughts with an '''apropos''' remark after a quarter of an hour's silence is really very showy and superficial. He had some analytical genius, no doubt; but he was by no means such a phenomenon as [[w:Edgar Allan Poe|{{quote-gloss|Edgar Allan}} Poe]] appeared to imagine."}}

